    {
      "angle": 0,
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "seed": 0,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 0,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "id": "t157",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1171.0,
      "y": 2609.375,
      "width": 558.0,
      "height": 281.25,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "text": "THE ONE-LINE CLAIM\n\nFusion weights conditioned on MEASURED IMAGE QUALITY produce\nbetter-ranked confidence than fixed-weight fusion, and that\nimprovement concentrates in the low-quality stratum.\n\n\nWHY THE GRID EXISTS\n\nThree heterogeneous models fused together ARE a deep ensemble,\nand deep ensembles are already a strong calibration baseline.\n\"our system is well calibrated\" therefore proves nothing.\nThe router is initialised to exactly uniform weights, so any\nB6 − B4 gap is attributable to what training learned from the\nquality features — not to a change of fusion family.",
      "originalText": "THE ONE-LINE CLAIM\n\nFusion weights conditioned on MEASURED IMAGE QUALITY produce\nbetter-ranked confidence than fixed-weight fusion, and that\nimprovement concentrates in the low-quality stratum.\n\n\nWHY THE GRID EXISTS\n\nThree heterogeneous models fused together ARE a deep ensemble,\nand deep ensembles are already a strong calibration baseline.\n\"our system is well calibrated\" therefore proves nothing.\nThe router is initialised to exactly uniform weights, so any\nB6 − B4 gap is attributable to what training learned from the\nquality features — not to a change of fusion family.",
      "fontSize": 15,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "r156",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": false,
      "roundness": null
    }
  ],
